  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows another shortwave trough moving northeast \nthrough Oklahoma currently. Forcing from this shortwave should \nresult in an increase in coverage of showers late this morning/early \nthis afternoon across eastern Kansas and west-central Missouri. This \nactivity then lifts to the north and east with time through the \nafternoon and evening hours. While it doesn't look like a washout, \nit does look like there will be isolated to scattered showers across \nthe area at some point through the day today/ this evening. So \nanyone doing outside activities may need an umbrella with them.\nFortunately, the chances for storms looks low with only weak  \ninstability available. Overnight, as that shortwave starts to \nmove northeast of the area and we move into the subsident \nregime, showers will decrease in coverage and eventually come to\nan end. Sunday, shortwave ridging builds through the area and \nthat should keep the area dry as the next system moves through \nthe Western US. \n\nThe Monday system is much stronger than the previous shortwaves. \nStrong southerly flow ahead of this system will result in very \nstrong moisture transport into the area. In fact, precipitable water \nvalues are at the max values for the NAEFS climatological period \nacross northeastern Kansas and northwestern Missouri. So this is 4 \nto 5 standard deviations above normal for this time of year. \nAdditionally, there is potential eastern Kansas and western Missouri \nare in the left front quadrant of the upper-jet moving into the area \nahead of the strong shortwave. Strong flow through much of the \ntroposphere will result in strong wind shear, potentially in excess \nof 50kts. Instability, though weak, is available in this strongly \nforced environment and we continue to have the potential for a high-\nshear, low-CAPE severe weather event. There are a couple scenarios \nthat cold unfold here. Storms could develop in the warm sector over \neastern Kansas, well ahead of the front. This looks possible,\nthough unlikely, based on some of the forecast soundings in \nthat area showing minimal or no CIN by the afternoon hours. A \nmore plausible scenario is that storms develop along or just \nahead of the front/ dryline well west of the area and then \ntrack east into the area later in the evening or overnight \nhours. There could also be some combination of both of these \nwith storms developing earlier in the warm sector, then a line \nof storms moving into the area in association with the front, \nthough this also seems less likely. Any one of these would have\nthe potential for strong to severe storms and given the strong \nkinematics, strong to damaging winds will be the most likely \nhazard. Strong 0-3km shear may also support a tornado risk as \nwell if a mature line of storms moves into the area. The latest \nDay 3 Convective Outlook brings a marginal risk of severe \nweather to eastern Kansas and western Missouri. A slight risk \nclips the extreme southwestern portions of the forecast area. \n\nIn the wake of this system, temperatures trend closer to normal for \nthe later half of next week. Normal highs are in the upper 50s so \neven though we cool down, highs still look to be a few degrees above \nnormal with highs forecast to be in the lower to middle 60s.",
